Deterministic Projection Algebra and Independence Calculus for Canonical Knowledge Ledgers

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This work specifies a formal architecture for a canonical, append-only knowledge ledger whose projections are deterministic under rebuild noise and whose epistemic transitions are gated by a computed independence relation rather than a declared provenance label. The central falsifiable hypothesis is a commutation condition: reconstruction from the ledger must factor through the projection function on the manifest-covered subledger, and null-rebuild control must pass before any hash comparison is interpreted as evidence of determinism. Three formal commitments distinguish the specification. First, the vector projection is stated over a symbolic state identity derived from stored embeddings under exact replay, with a separate tolerance contract for re-encoding under a guard band; the two contracts are not conflated. Second, bitemporal determinism is enforced through valid-time exclusion and transaction-time continuity under an explicit application-level serialization discipline, because the temporal projection requires a total order to be well-defined. Third, independence is computed as a dependency-closure property over a provenance graph, with same-root evidence collapsed before any corroboration tally, and the promotion gate is stated as a threshold-admissibility condition rather than a variance discount. No experimental result is claimed; the contribution is a falsifiable specification whose correctness conditions can be tested.

---

1. Research Question and Formal Hypothesis

1.1 Objects

Let $\mathcal{L}$ be the canonical ledger, represented as a finite set of typed relations over an append-only domain. Let $\mathcal{G}$, $\mathcal{V}$, $\mathcal{T}$ be the graph, vector, and temporal projections respectively. Let $\mathcal{R}$ be the reconstruction procedure. A projection is a function

P : \Sigma_{\mathcal{L}} \to \Sigma_{P}

where $\Sigma_{\mathcal{L}}$ is the space of canonical ledger states and $\Sigma_{P}$ is the space of projection states. The projection is deterministic if for every ledger state $L \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{L}}$, $P(L)$ is unique. Determinism is a property of the projection specification, not of the storage substrate.

1.2 Central hypothesis

\mathcal{R}_{P} \circ \iota = \pi_{P}

on the manifest-covered subledger, where $\iota : \Sigma_{\mathcal{L}} \to \Sigma_{\mathcal{L}}$ is the identity on the ledger restricted to the reconstruction manifest’s covered subledger and $\pi_{P}$ is the projection function. The hash condition is the corollary

\sigma_{P}\bigl(\mathcal{R}_{P}(L)\bigr) = \sigma_{P}\bigl(\pi_{P}(L)\bigr).

If coverage is partial, the diagram commutes only on the covered subledger and the reconstruction procedure must return a partial equivalence witness—the covered set and the uncovered set—rather than a binary pass/fail.

Falsifiability condition. The equality is meaningful only if it holds on a null rebuild—a rebuild with no change to any canonical input. If the null-rebuild hash differs from the baseline, the determinism contract is violated and no subsequent hash comparison can be interpreted. The null-rebuild control is therefore a precondition of the benchmark, not a result of it. A null rebuild must vary execution environment, batch size, and reduction order; two reconstructions in the same environment prove nothing about determinism under rebuild noise.

1.3 Scope boundary

The formalism begins after extraction. Nondeterminism in automatic speech recognition, optical character recognition, and other upstream extractors is outside the projection contract. The ledger records extraction artifacts; it does not claim to make extraction deterministic. This boundary must be stated explicitly wherever a projection hash is interpreted as evidence about the ledger rather than about the extractor.

