3. The Canonical Ledger

3.1 Ledger signature

\mathcal{L} = (\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M})

where $\mathcal{K}$ is the set of KEU identities, $\mathcal{R}$ is the set of immutable content revisions, $\mathcal{E}$ is the set of epistemic events, $\mathcal{A}$ is the set of independence assessments, and $\mathcal{M}$ is the set of projection reconstruction manifests. The outbox is a transport artifact subject to retention policy and is not a reconstruction source.

3.2 Bitemporal domain

A bitemporal KEU revision is a tuple

\rho = \bigl(\mathrm{vid}, v, [t_{s}, t_{e}), [\tau_{r}, \tau_{s}), \ldots \bigr)

where $v$ is the revision number, $[t_{s}, t_{e})$ is the valid-time interval during which the proposition is asserted to hold, and $[\tau_{r}, \tau_{s})$ is the transaction-time interval during which this revision is the current recorded version, with $\tau_{r} = \texttt{recorded\_at}$ and $\tau_{s} = \texttt{superseded\_at}$.

Invariant V1 (valid-time exclusion). For any two revisions $\rho_{a}$, $\rho_{b}$ of the same KEU identity that are concurrently current in transaction time, the valid-time intervals do not overlap:

[t_{s}^{a}, t_{e}^{a}) \cap [t_{s}^{b}, t_{e}^{b}) = \varnothing.

The qualification “concurrently current in transaction time” is essential: a correction to a valid-time interval is a legitimate operation, and must be expressible as a supersession that makes the corrected revision current while the superseded revision remains historical. V1 without the qualification forbids correction.

Invariant T1 (transaction-time continuity). For consecutive revisions $v$ and $v+1$ of the same KEU identity,

\tau_{s}^{(v)} = \tau_{r}^{(v+1)}.

The supersession chain is gap-free. This makes the transaction-time total order well-defined for replay.

Concurrency discipline. V1 is enforceable through a PostgreSQL exclusion constraint over the valid-time range, keyed by KEU identity. PostgreSQL 18’s WITHOUT OVERLAPS covers a single range column, and for two time axes the established pattern is an EXCLUDE constraint over both ranges. Neither mechanism alone resolves concurrent inserts of intervals that individually satisfy V1 but jointly violate it: two transactions can each insert a non-overlapping interval without seeing the other’s uncommitted row. The insertion path must acquire an exclusive lock on the KEU identity before attempting the insert—SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the identity row or a per-identity advisory lock held for the transaction. T1 is not expressible as a declarative range constraint and must be enforced by a constraint trigger or a previous-pointer chain under the same lock. Ordering must not depend on wall-clock timestamps or sequence values, both of which can be made visible out of order or can contain permanent gaps; the transaction position is assigned by the ledger under the identity lock and recorded as the ordering key.

3.3 Ledger invariants

L1 (Revision immutability). The tuple $(\texttt{content}, \texttt{media\_start\_ms}, \texttt{media\_end\_ms}, \texttt{extraction\_method}, \texttt{producer\_agent})$ is write-once. Supersession updates only $\texttt{superseded\_at}$ and the historical status marker; it does not mutate content.

L2 (Deferred epistemic-event existence). Every revision that is not the terminal superseded revision of its KEU identity eventually acquires at least one epistemic event whose revision field matches. The constraint is deferred to the transaction boundary, not required at revision insertion, so that a revision can exist in an epistemically inert state before an event is attached. This makes $\mathrm{UNDEFINED}$ reachable and observable, rather than excluded by construction.

L3 (Status transition consistency). For every epistemic event with a non-null new status, the previous status equals the new status of the immediately preceding event for the same revision under the ledger ordering, or is null if the event is first.

L4 (Manifest coverage). A projection $P$ is reconstructible at ledger state $L$ iff there exists a manifest $M_{P}(L)$ listing, for every canonical revision contributing to $P(L)$, the required encoder artifacts, transformation versions, and serialization specifications. The coverage of $P$ at $L$ is

\mathrm{cov}_{P}(L) = \frac{|\{\text{contributing revisions covered by } M_{P}(L)\}|}{|\{\text{contributing revisions at } L\}|}.

A projection with $\mathrm{cov}_{P}(L) < 1$ is partially reconstructible, and the reconstruction procedure reports the uncovered set.

L5 (Commit-time epistemic stratification). The effective epistemic status of a revision is not stored as a mutable column. It is computed as the endpoint of the unique maximal event chain for that revision under the ledger ordering. Projection workers read the computed status, never a cached field. A revision with no event chain is epistemically inert: it may exist in the ledger, but it cannot contribute to a verified graph edge or a corroboration tally. This mirrors the commit-time status discipline of Eigenius, adapted to a ledger in which status transitions are events rather than column updates.

