4. Deterministic Projection Algebra

4.1 Projection definition

A projection $P$ is a triple $(\Sigma_{P}, \pi_{P}, \sigma_{P})$ where $\Sigma_{P}$ is the projection state space, $\pi_{P} : \Sigma_{\mathcal{L}} \to \Sigma_{P}$ is the projection function, and $\sigma_{P} : \Sigma_{P} \to \{0,1\}^{256}$ is the projection identity function

\sigma_{P}(S) = H_{\nu}\bigl(\mathsf{ser}_{\nu}(S)\bigr).

$P$ is deterministic iff $\sigma_{P}(\pi_{P}(L)) = \sigma_{P}(\pi_{P}(L'))$ whenever $L$ and $L'$ are canonically equivalent ledger states—states differing only in non-semantic metadata such as arrival order, transaction identifiers not referenced by the projection, and physical storage layout.

4.2 The vector projection: two contracts, not one

A single vector contract cannot serve both exact replay and re-encoding. The specification therefore separates them.

Exact-replay contract. When the original embedding artifacts are stored and replayed without re-encoding, the projection state is the set of stored vectors keyed by stable identifier,

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{replay}}(L) = \bigl\{ (\mathrm{id}_{i}, \mathrm{bytes}(e_{i})) \;\big|\; e_{i} \text{ stored in } L \bigr\},

and the projection identity is the hash of the canonically serialized set. This contract is bit-exact and falsifiable: a null replay with the same artifact bytes must produce the same hash, and a differing hash indicates a serialization or assembly defect, not a semantic change.

Re-encoding contract. When the embedding must be regenerated—because the artifact is unavailable or because a downstream task requires a fresh encoding—the projection state is the symbolic row set

\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sym}}(L) = \bigl\{ (\mathrm{id}_{i}, \kappa(e_{i})) \;\big|\; e_{i} \text{ is an embedding recorded in } L \bigr\},

where $\kappa : \mathbb{R}^{d} \to \mathcal{Q}^{m}$ is a fixed deterministic quantizer into a finite alphabet. The re-encoding contract is not hash equality. It is a tolerance condition against a floor calibrated from null re-encodings, and the floor must be measured across environments, not within one.

The boundary problem. Proposition 1 in its naive form—quantization makes the hash invariant under rebuild noise—is vacuous. The proof says the hash is unchanged whenever noise does not cross a bin boundary, which is the claim itself. A point lying arbitrarily close to a quantization boundary incurs the full symbolic jump for an arbitrarily small perturbation, and the flip probability grows with the number of rows and symbolic dimensions relative to the bin width. A whole-corpus hash over a single quantizer will therefore fail under realistic rebuild noise even when no semantic change has occurred.

The resolution is twofold. First, per-row hashes are stored under a Merkle tree, so that a differing root localizes to differing rows rather than forcing a whole-state pass/fail. Second, a guard band is declared: rows within a margin of a bin boundary are flagged at encoding time and are excluded from the tolerance comparison, with their identities reported explicitly. A candidate re-encoding passes the tolerance gate iff the flagged-row set is unchanged and every unflagged row satisfies

d_{H}\bigl(\kappa(e_{i}), \kappa(e_{i}')\bigr) \le \Phi + \varepsilon,

where $\Phi$ is the floor observed across environments and $\varepsilon$ is a declared margin. Rows exceeding the floor are reported as genuine changes, not absorbed into noise. Multiple quantizers may be used to reduce the flagged set, but they do not eliminate the boundary phenomenon and must be stated as part of the encoding contract.

Proposition 1 (Replay determinism). Under the exact-replay contract, if the stored embedding bytes and the serialization version are fixed, $\sigma_{\mathcal{V}}$ is a deterministic function of the ledger state, and a null replay with identical bytes yields identical identity.

Proof. The projection state is a set of identifier–byte pairs. Canonical serialization is a function of that set alone, and SHA-256 is a function of its input. No reduction order, device, or batch size enters the replay path. $\square$

Proposition 2 (Re-encoding is not bit-deterministic). There is no finite quantization scheme and no non-zero noise bound under which the whole-corpus re-encoding identity is guaranteed invariant.

Proof sketch. For any bin partition, there exist embedding coordinates arbitrarily close to a boundary. A perturbation smaller than the bin width can cross the boundary and change the symbol. Since a corpus contains finitely many rows, the probability of at least one crossing is positive for any non-zero noise, and grows with corpus size. $\square$

The re-encoding contract is therefore a tolerance contract with a flagged set, not a hash contract. Conflating the two contracts produces a specification that is either unfalsifiable (if the tolerance is read as a hash) or unsatisfiable (if the hash is read as a tolerance).

4.3 The graph projection and edge stratification

The graph projection state is $\mathcal{G}(L) = (V_{\mathcal{G}}, E_{\mathcal{G}})$ where nodes are revisions and edges are typed relations. Each edge carries a stratification tag in $\{\mathrm{DERIVED\_FROM}, \mathrm{SUPPORTS}, \mathrm{CAUSES}\}$.

Edge admissibility. An edge $e = (u, v, \mathrm{CAUSES})$ is admissible only if both endpoint statuses are defined, there exists a supporting evidence record whose proved proposition covers the causal proposition and whose KEU identity matches $u$, and the causal proposition itself has an epistemic event with a new status in $\{\mathrm{CLAIMED}, \mathrm{OBSERVED}, \mathrm{CORROBORATED}\}$. The existence of two verified nodes does not license a causal edge; the edge is itself a proposition requiring evidence and status. The graph identity is the hash of the node set and the edge set sorted by $(\mathrm{source\_id}, \mathrm{target\_id}, \mathrm{relation\_type})$, with each edge serialization including its stratification tag and supporting evidence identifiers.

4.4 The temporal projection

\mathcal{T}(L) = \bigl\{ (\mathrm{keu\_id}, v, [t_{s}, t_{e}), [\tau_{r}, \tau_{s})) \bigr\}

serialized in ledger-assigned transaction order, with ties broken by revision number. Under V1 with the concurrency qualification and T1, the serialization order is uniquely determined, and $\sigma_{\mathcal{T}}$ is a deterministic function of $L$. Without the qualification, corrections would be unrepresentable; without T1, the transaction order would be partial and the hash not well-defined.

