6. Epistemic Event Calculus

6.1 Status lattice

\mathcal{S} = \{\bot, \mathrm{CLAIMED}, \mathrm{OBSERVED}, \mathrm{CORROBORATED}\}

with the partial order

\bot \sqsubset \mathrm{CLAIMED} \sqsubset \mathrm{CORROBORATED}, \qquad
\bot \sqsubset \mathrm{OBSERVED} \sqsubset \mathrm{CORROBORATED}.

$\mathrm{CLAIMED}$ and $\mathrm{OBSERVED}$ are incomparable. $\mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ is the unique top.

Lattice admissibility. A transition $s \to s'$ with $s' \neq s$ is admissible iff $s \sqsubset s'$ and, when $s' = \mathrm{CORROBORATED}$, the gate of §5.4 passes. The transition $\mathrm{CLAIMED} \to \mathrm{OBSERVED}$ is not a lattice refinement, because the two statuses are incomparable; it is recorded as a kind change with its own event type, not as a warrant increase, and it does not move the revision upward in the lattice. This resolves the inconsistency between a lattice-order transition rule and the operational need to record a change in the kind of evidence.

6.2 Event algebra

An epistemic event is a tuple $e = (r, s_{\mathrm{prev}}, s_{\mathrm{new}}, \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \eta)$ where $r$ is the revision, $\alpha$ is the assessment reference or $\bot$, $\beta$ is the corroboration reference set, $\gamma$ is the corroboration scope, and $\eta$ is the hash of the gate’s inputs—the collapsed root set, the $\rho_{\mathrm{err}}$ estimate or the conservative-choice marker, and the support relation used. Composition $\circ$ is defined iff the codomain status of the first event equals the domain status of the second. Under L3, every revision’s event history is a path in the lattice starting at $\bot$.

6.3 The gate is not a pure function of the ledger

The promotion gate reads the provenance graph and the dependence estimate, both of which can change after a $\mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ event is recorded. A later-discovered common ancestor or a revised $\rho_{\mathrm{err}}$ can invalidate the gate’s premises. The event must therefore carry $\eta$, and the ledger must admit a defeat event that revokes or downgrades a prior corroboration when the gate’s inputs change. Stale corroboration without defeat is a silent integrity failure, and Non-claim 2—the incompleteness of the provenance graph—is precisely the condition under which it occurs. The defeat event is not an appendix to the calculus; it is a structural requirement of a gate whose inputs are not frozen at commit time.

6.4 Proposition 5 (No silent promotion)

Under L2 and L3, no revision can attain effective status $\mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ without an event whose admissibility was checked by the gate of §5.4.

Proof. Effective status is the endpoint of the event history. L2 guarantees that every revision that is not terminal-superseded eventually acquires an event history; L3 guarantees that the history is a lattice path. An event with $s_{\mathrm{new}} = \mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ is inserted only if the gate returns true; otherwise the transaction aborts. Therefore no revision reaches $\mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ without passing the gate. $\square$

