Appendix F: Relation to Prior Art — Detailed Comparison

F.1 SEMQ

SEMQ encodes unit-norm float32 vectors into quantized symbolic rows, derives a whole-state identity from the row set, measures a floor from null rebuilds, and diffs candidate builds against that floor row by row. Its conformance vectors pin byte-identical behaviour across Python, Rust, Go, and TypeScript bindings. The re-encoding contract of §4.2 is semantically aligned with SEMQ’s Encoding/state_id/Floor/diff pipeline. The contribution of the present specification is not the quantized identity itself but the separation of the replay contract from the re-encoding contract, the guard-band flagged set, and the integration of the vector projection into a ledger-level determinism contract with coverage reporting. If the prototype does not adopt SEMQ directly, it must provide a comparative justification and a test case where its quantizer succeeds and SEMQ fails; without that, the reinvention is unjustified.

F.2 Eigenius

Eigenius enforces epistemic status (declared/observed/derived/verified) as a strict commit-time invariant over a typed knowledge-graph DBMS, with status computed from the provenance graph and “verified” carrying a formally re-checked proof term via Lean 4. The present specification adopts the commit-time discipline (L5) but names its top category $\mathrm{CORROBORATED}$ because the gate establishes human-assessed corroboration under a computed independence relation, which is weaker than a checked proof term. The naming difference is a claim difference: Eigenius’s verified category warrants a stronger assertion than the gate of §5.4 can support.

F.3 Evidence Laundering

The EMNLP 2026 study evaluates a content-only attack in which one false claim is published across paraphrased pages that appear to be independent sources, and shows that agents assign laundered clusters credence comparable to genuine independence controls; a structural provenance gate substantially reduces the failure. Its limitations are adopted here as declared bounds: plain word-overlap deduplication merges genuinely independent pages, and independently rewritten false pages are largely not collapsed. The collapse operator of §5.2 is only as good as the recorded derivation edges, and the benchmark must measure recall on the adaptive paraphrase test (T10) rather than assume it.

F.4 Bayesian source dependence

The VLDB 2009 work applies Bayesian analysis to decide dependence between sources, iteratively detecting copying and discovering truth from conflicting information, and extends the model to source accuracy and value similarity. The structural collapse of §5.2 is an auditable, transaction-time approximation to this problem; the Bayesian formulation is the reference model against which the $\rho_{\mathrm{err}}$ estimate should be calibrated. Co-copying and transitive copying remain harder cases for the structural operator, because both require edges that may not be recorded.

F.5 Bitemporal event logs

XTDB-style and Datomic-style bitemporal event logs are the natural baseline for the temporal projection. The contribution of the present specification is not the bitemporal model itself but the combination of valid-time exclusion under an explicit concurrency discipline, transaction-time continuity enforced by a trigger or pointer chain, and a reconstruction manifest that makes the projection replayable with coverage reporting. The comparison benchmark must include a bitemporal event log baseline, not only an internal null rebuild.

